 "id": "2026-chen-community",
 "name": "Community Park Scenes: Walking Intention and Feelings",
 "description": "Faithful replication with the paper's own stimuli: Chen and Jiang (2026, Scientific Reports 16: 17659) asked which visual attributes of community parks make older adults want to walk there and feel better. From 200 photographs of parks in Xiamen, Nanchang and Yancheng they chose eight, two contrasting levels each of green-space accessibility (a path into the lawn or not), colour hue (warm vs cool flower fields), vegetation enclosure and artificiality (ornamental water and art). 52 adults aged 60–69 viewed each photo for 30 s between grey rest screens while skin conductance was recorded, then rated walking-intention items and positive and negative affect on a 4-point scale. Less enclosed spaces gave larger emotional benefits but lower walking intention, accessibility raised intention, artificiality lowered both, and cool hues beat warm ones. This template uses the authors' eight photographs from Zenodo (10.5281/zenodo.18772183, CC BY 4.0) in folder \"parks\" (gsa1/gsa2, hue1/hue2, enclosure1/enclosure2, artificial1/artificial2): a 60-s grey rest, then for each photo in random order 30 s of grey, 30 s of the photo full screen, and five 4-point items (appreciate the scenery, inclined to walk here, would visit often if near home, positive emotions, uncomfortable feelings), plus the two walking-habit items, age, gender and urban/rural background.\n\nDifferences from the original study: (1) no skin conductance or other physiological recording; (2) photo order is random for each participant instead of 12 counterbalanced sequences; (3) the before-photos control questionnaire uses two self-written items (\"Right now I feel positive emotions / uncomfortable\"); (4) the visit-frequency item is reworded in plain English from the paper's Table 3 (\"If the depicted location were proximal to my residence, it would exhibit elevated visitation frequency for ambulatory activities\"); (5) the two walking-habit items are asked once at the start, not with every photo; (6) open to any adult online, not only 60–69-year-olds tested at home; (7) photos are resized to 1600 px.",
